  "kind": "building",
  "notes": "Shipyard (8×12, D-054; water-edge: the back four rows, -y, stand over the sea). Structure, not slab: a long timber-framed building hall under thatch stands at the head of the slipway, its big doors open to the slip; the slipway runs from the hall across the four water rows on piles and carries a hull in progress (keel, stem and sternpost, ribs, the lower strakes planked) between staging; a treadwheel crane with its jib over the slip at the water's edge. On the right a timber yard with log piles, plank and beam stacks, a saw pit, ballast, an anchor, rope and barrels, and a jetty with a rowing boat over the water rows. The slip anchor is where the client raises the ordered hull.",
